Learning a second language improves executive function, delays dementia onset by 4–5 years, and improves performance on unrelated cognitive tasks — the benefits transfer across domains.
The 'growth mindset' (Dweck, Stanford) — the belief that abilities can be developed through effort — is measurably more predictive of academic and professional success than fixed-ability beliefs.
Retirement studies consistently show that people who engage in learning activities after retiring maintain sharper cognitive function and live longer than those who do not.
